'''''Myriopteris aurea''''', the '''golden lip fern''' or '''Bonaire lip fern''', is a medium-sized fern native to the [[Americas]], a member of the family [[Pteridaceae]]. Unlike many members of its genus, which are relatively lacy, its leaf is only dissected into lobed leaflets (pinnae), which are hairy both above and below. One of the [[cheilanthoid]] [[lip fern]]s, it was classified in the genus ''[[Cheilanthes]]'' as '''''Cheilanthes bonariensis''''' until 2013, when the genus ''[[Myriopteris]]'' was again recognized as separate from ''Cheilanthes''. It typically grows on dry, rocky slopes, and ranges from the southwestern United States through [[Mexico]], where it is common and widespread, south and east through Central and South America as far as [[Chile]] and [[Argentina]]. The common names refer to the color of the hairs on the upper surface of the leaves, and to early collections around [[Buenos Aires]], Latinized as "Bonaire".

==Description==
Leaf bases are closely spaced along the horizontal [[rhizome]], which is variously described as being {{convert|3|mm|in|sigfig=1|sp=us}}{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}} or {{convert|4|to|8|mm|in|sigfig=1|sp=us}} in diameter.{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}} The rhizome bears scales, which are [[Glossary of leaf morphology#linear|linear]] to [[Glossary of leaf morphology#lanceolate|lanceolate]], with [[Glossary of leaf morphology#entire|entire]] (untoothed){{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=181}}{{sfn|Tryon|Stolze|1989|p=28}} or subentire (very slightly toothed) margins.{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}} They are bi-colored, with a shiny central stripe red-brown{{sfn|Lellinger|1985|p=154}} or shiny chestnut-brown{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}} to black in color{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}} and narrow light-brown margins,{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}} and measure {{convert|3|mm|in|sigfig=1|sp=us}} long.{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}} They are slightly twisted and strongly pressed against the rhizome.{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}}

The fronds spring up in clusters;{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}} unlike many ferns, they do not unfold as fiddleheads (noncircinate [[vernation]]). {{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}}{{sfn|Grusz|Windham|Yatskievych|Huiet|2014|p=705}} They range from {{convert|10|to|60|cm|in|sp=us|sigfig=2}} long,{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}}{{sfn|Tryon|Stolze|1989|p=28}} sometimes up to {{convert|75|cm|in|sp=us|sigfig=2}},{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}} and are {{convert|0.5|to|3.5|cm|in|sigfig=2|sp=us}} broad.{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}} The [[stipe (botany)|stipe]] (the stalk of the leaf, below the blade) represents from one-sixth to one-third of the total length of the leaf,{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}}{{sfn|Tryon|Stolze|1989|p=28}} and is {{convert|3|to|15|cm|in|sigfig=2|sp=us}} long.{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}} The upper surface of the stipe is rounded and not grooved.{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}}{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}}{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}} The stipe is shiny, dark chestnut brown to black{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}}{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}} or purplish-black in color.{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}}{{sfn|Lellinger|1985|p=154}} It is covered in straight, white to tan hairs, {{convert|2|mm|in|sigfig=1|sp=us}} long and more or less pressed against the stipe.{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}}{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}}

The leaf blades are linear to lanceolate{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}} or elliptical,{{sfn|Tryon|Stolze|1989|p=28}} and [[pinnation|pinnate-pinnatifid]] (cut into deeply lobed pinnae),{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}}{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}} much less dissected than most ''Myriopteris'' species.{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=178}} It is from {{convert|1|to|4|cm|in|sp=us|sigfig=1}} wide.{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}} The [[rachis]] (leaf axis) is densely covered in pubescent hairs, but lacks scales.{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}}{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}} From 15 to 44&nbsp;pairs of pinnae are present,{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}} attached directly to the rachis or with a short stalk.{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}} Each pinna is approximately equilateral in shape,{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}} has from 3 to 8&nbsp;pairs of lobes, which may be cut as shallowly as one-fourth or as deeply as three-fourths of the distance to the [[costa (botany)|costa]] (pinna axis). The number of lobes and deepness of cutting can vary a great deal between individuals.{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}} They are joined to the rachis by a distinct stalk; the dark color of the rachis extends into the stalk but terminates abruptly in a swollen node covered with hairs.{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}} The lowest pair of pinnae is slightly smaller than the one above.{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}} At the other end of the frond, the pinnae gradually taper to an acute or obtuse apex.{{sfn|Lellinger|1985|p=154}} The leaf tissue is parchment-like to almost leathery.{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}} The upper surface of the pinnae is covered with scattered to abundant hairs, stiff, flattened against the surface, one-celled, and about {{convert|2|mm|in|sigfig=1|sp=us}} long.{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}} They are pale golden-tan in color.{{sfn|Lellinger|1985|p=154}}{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}} The lower surface of the pinnae is also covered in hairs, matted so thickly as to hide the leaf tissue, which vary from white, particularly when young,{{sfn|Tryon|Stolze|1989|p=28}} to rusty red in color.{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}}{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}} They do not curl up when dried out.{{sfn|Lellinger|1985|p=154}}

On fertile fronds, the [[sorus|sori]] are located at the ends of [[vein (botany)|vein]]s near the margin of the leaf,{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}}{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}} forming a more or less continuous zone adjacent to the margin,{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}} which curls back slightly but does not form a distinct false [[indusium]] to protect them.{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}} The recurved margin is 0.05 to 0.25&nbsp;mm wide.{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}} It is somewhat thinner and more delicate than the rest of the leaf tissue, though not quite [[hyaline]].{{sfn|Tryon|Stolze|1989|p=28}}{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}} Each [[sporangium]] contains 32 dark brown to black spores.{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}}{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}} The vast majority of ''M.&nbsp;aurea'' individuals thus far examined are [[apogamous]] [[triploid]]s, with a chromosome number of 90 present in both [[sporophyte]] and [[gametophyte]].{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}}{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}} A few populations forming 64 spores per sporangium have reportedly been found, and are presumed to be sexual [[diploid]]s.{{sfn|Grusz|Windham|Yatskievych|Huiet|2014|pp=707–708, 711}}

Among its [[congener (biology)|congener]]s ''M.&nbsp;aurea'' is most similar to ''[[Myriopteris yatskievychiana|M.&nbsp;yatskievychiana]]'', known only from [[Sonora]] which is smaller and has dense white (rather than rusty) hairs on the underside of the leaf.{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=213}} The pinnate-pinnatifid leaf blades distinguish these two species from the rest of the genus, the other species being more highly dissected.{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=178}} ''M.&nbsp;aurea'' superficially resembles some species of ''[[Astrolepis]]'', such as ''[[Astrolepis sinuata|A.&nbsp;sinuata]]'', which have short, deeply lobed pinnae, but they bear the stellate scales that give their genus its name rather than the hairs seen on ''M.&nbsp;aurea''.{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}} In the southern part of its range, it might be confused with ''[[Cheilanthes fraseri]]'', but the latter has leaves widely spaced along the rhizome, pinnae more or less triangular (rather than equilateral), and rhizome scales light brown at the apex, rather than dark brown.{{sfn|Tryon|Stolze|1989|pp=23,28}}

==Taxonomy==
The species was first [[species description|described]] as ''Pteris aurea'' by [[Jean Louis Marie Poiret]] in [[Jean-Baptiste Lamarck|Lamarck]]'s [[Encyclopédie Méthodique]], Botanique in 1804. He based his description on a specimen collected in [[Peru]] by [[Joseph de Jussieu]].{{sfn|Poiret|1804|p=710}} This [[type specimen]] (sheet 1333 of Jussieu's herbarium) is at the [[Paris Herbarium]].{{sfn|Grusz|Windham|2013|p=56}} The [[specific epithet]] ''aurea'', meaning "golden",{{sfn|Short|George|2013|p=127}} evidently refers to the "yellow, almost golden" hairs covering the upper surface of the leaves.{{sfn|Poiret|1804|p=710}}

[[File:Myriopteris_aurea_(helecho_dorado)_II.jpg|thumb|left|200px|Closeup showing the covering of hairs on the leaf, very dense on the underside|alt=The underside of one pinnate fern frond with lobed pinnae, showing dense white hairs; in the background is the upper side of a larger frond, showing similar but sparser hairs]]

In 1810, [[Carl Ludwig Willdenow]] recognized ''Pteris aurea'' in the 5th edition of ''[[Species Plantarum]]'',{{sfn|Willdenow|1810|p=365}} but also described a new species, ''Acrostichum bonariense'', based on material collected near [[Buenos Aires]] (Latinized as "Bonaria"), an illustration of which was published by [[Johann Amman]] in 1738.{{sfn|Willdenow|1810|pp=114–115}}{{sfn|Amman|1738|pp=300–301}} Willdenow evidently did not have access to any of Amman's material, and so failed to recognize the similarity between Amman's fern and ''Pteris aurea''.{{sfn|Ponce|Zimmer|2011}} Confusion was compounded in 1822, when [[Johann Heinrich Friedrich Link|Heinrich Friedrich Link]] described the species yet again, based on a [[Alexander von Humboldt|Humboldt]] and [[Aimé Bonpland|Bonpland]] specimen which Willdenow had labeled ''Cheilanthes ferruginea'' but had omitted from ''Species Plantarum''.{{sfn|Link|1822|p=463}} The specific epithet ''ferruginea'' means "rusty-reddish",{{sfn|Short|George|2013|p=171}} perhaps referring to the color of the hairs below, so described by Willdenow for ''A.&nbsp;bonariense''.{{sfn|Willdenow|1810|p=114}}

In his ''Reliquae Haenkeanae'' in 1825, [[Carl Borivoj Presl]] recognized that ''A.&nbsp;bonariensis'' and ''C.&nbsp;ferruginea'' were the same species, but (illegitimately) invented the new name of ''Notholaena rufa'' to encompass both.{{sfn|Presl|1825|p=19}} Placement in ''Notholaena'' reflected the placement of the [[sorus|sori]] at or near the margin of the leaf, combined with an absence of a distinct false [[indusium]] formed by that margin.{{sfn|Presl|1825|p=18}} The specific epithet ''rufa'', meaning "reddish",{{sfn|Short|George|2013|p=245}} corresponds to his description of the color of hairs on both sides of the leaf. Unfortunately, Presl also included the farinose ''N.&nbsp;ferruginea'' (Desv.) Desv. and ''N.&nbsp;tomentosa'' Desv. (now considered ''[[Notholaena trichomanoides|N.&nbsp;trichomanoides]]'' and ''[[Cheilanthes hypoleuca|C.&nbsp;hypoleuca]]'', respectively) within his concept of the species. He distinguished between var. ''minor'' of the species, with dense hairs on the upper surface, and var. ''major'', with sparse hairs on the upper surface.{{sfn|Presl|1825|p=19}} Meanwhile, [[Nicaise Auguste Desvaux]] also recognized ''Notholaena'', and transferred ''P.&nbsp;aurea'' there as ''N.&nbsp;aurea'' in 1827.{{sfn|Desvaux|1827|p=219}} [[Christian Gottfried Daniel Nees von Esenbeck|Christian Nees von Esenbeck]] transferred ''C.&nbsp;ferruginea'' to ''[[Pellaea (plant)|Pellaea]]'' as ''P.&nbsp;ferruginea'' in 1847,{{sfn|Nees von Esenbeck|1847|p=684}} but this was not widely followed.

[[William Jackson Hooker]] transferred ''C.&nbsp;ferruginea'' to ''[[Notholaena]]'' in his ''Species Filicum'' in 1864, and recognized the distinctness of ''N.&nbsp;trichomanoides'', but his use of the name ''N.&nbsp;ferruginea'' was illegitimate as it had already been used by Desvaux, and he failed to recognize ''N.&nbsp;tomentosa'' as a distinct species.{{sfn|Hooker|1864|p=108}} Hooker's re-use of ''N.&nbsp;ferruginea'' for this species led to continued confusion with ''N.&nbsp;trichomanoides''.{{sfn|Weatherby|1939|p=21}} In 1906 [[Carl Christensen (botanist)|Carl Christensen]] transferred the more senior ''A.&nbsp;bonariensis'' to ''Notholaena'' as ''Notholaena bonariensis'', but recognized both ''N.&nbsp;ferruginea'' (Desv.) Desv. and ''N.&nbsp;tomentosa'' as synonyms.{{sfn|Christensen|1906|p=459}} This confusion appears to have led José N. Rovirosa to found another name for the species, ''Notholaena chiapensis'', named for one of his collections in [[Chiapas]]; he noted that it was non-farinose, and thought that Hooker's name applied to the farinose species (i.e., ''N.&nbsp;trichomanoides'').{{sfn|Rovirosa|1909|p=229}} It was not until 1939 that [[Charles Alfred Weatherby]], who was looking for [[type specimens]] in Desvaux's collections at the Paris herbarium, identified ''Notholaena aurea'' as the most senior name for the species in that genus and unraveled the erroneous synonymies.{{sfn|Weatherby|1939|p=21}}

In 1953, [[George R. Proctor]] transferred the species to ''[[Cheilanthes]]'' as ''Cheilanthes bonariensis'', since the name ''Cheilanthes aurea'' was preoccupied.{{sfn|Proctor|1953|p=15}} However, most authors continued to place it in ''Notholaena''.{{sfn|Ponce|Zimmer|2011}} One of these was [[Rolla M. Tryon Jr.]] who, in 1956, published a revision of American ''Notholaena'' incorporating material from Weatherby, who had died in 1949. Tryon accepted the species in ''Notholaena'' as ''N.&nbsp;aurea'', and circumscribed it in the fashion presently accepted (including ''A.&nbsp;bonariensis'', ''C.&nbsp;ferruginea'', and ''N.&nbsp;chiapensis'', and excluding ''N.&nbsp;ferruginea'' (Desv.) Desv. and ''N.&nbsp;tomentosa'' Desv.). He accepted as the type of ''A.&nbsp;bonariensis'' a specimen seen by Weatherby in Willdenow's herbarium in [[Berlin Botanical Garden|Berlin]].{{sfn|Tryon|Weatherby|1956|p=35}}

Tryon noted that the generic delimitation of ''Notholaena'' and ''Cheilanthes'', based on the traditional criterion that the latter had leaf edges curled under and modified into distinct false indusia while the former did not, was unsatisfactory, given the existence of many intermediate forms. In 1982, he and his wife [[Alice F. Tryon|Alice]] published an extended survey of the ferns in which they narrowed the [[circumscription (taxonomy)|circumscription]] of ''Notholaena'', treating ''N.&nbsp;aurea'' as ''C.&nbsp;bonariensis''.{{sfn|Tryon|Tryon|1982}} This classification was widely taken up by other workers.{{sfn|Ponce|Zimmer|2011}} Research at the Berlin herbarium subsequently revealed that the specimen in Willdenow's herbarium came from Mexico, and did not match his original description, registering it ineligible as a type. In 2011, M. Mónica Ponce and Brigitte Zimmer designated Amman's illustration as the [[lectotype]] of ''A.&nbsp;bonariense''; as there was some question as to whether the drawing represented ''C.&nbsp;bonariensis'' in the conventional sense or ''[[Cheilanthes buchtienii]]'', they further designated Willdenow's Mexican specimen as an [[epitype]].{{sfn|Ponce|Zimmer|2011}}

Meanwhile, the development of [[molecular phylogenetics|molecular phylogenetic]] methods showed that the traditional circumscription of ''Cheilanthes'' is [[polyphyletic]]. [[Convergent evolution]] in arid environments is thought to be responsible for widespread [[homoplasy]] in the morphological characters traditionally used to classify it and the segregate genera that have sometimes been recognized. On the basis of molecular evidence, Amanda Grusz and [[Michael D. Windham]] revived the genus ''[[Myriopteris]]'' in 2013 for a group of species formerly placed in ''Cheilanthes''. One of these was ''C.&nbsp;bonariensis''; since the epithet ''aurea'' was not preoccupied, they transferred ''P.&nbsp;aurea'' to the genus to become ''Myriopteris aurea''.{{sfn|Grusz|Windham|2013}}

In 2018, [[Maarten J. M. Christenhusz]] transferred the species to ''[[Hemionitis]]'' as ''H.&nbsp;bonariensis'' (''H.&nbsp;aurea'' being preoccupied), as part of a program to consolidate the cheilanthoid ferns into that genus.{{sfn|Christenhusz|Fay|Byng|2018|p=10}}

Members of the genus ''Cheilanthes'' as historically defined (which includes ''Myriopteris'') are commonly known as "lip ferns" due to the lip-like (false) indusium formed by the leaf margins curling over the sori.{{sfn|Clute|1901|pp=237,242}} The common names '''golden lip fern''' and '''Bonaire lip fern'''{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}}{{sfn|Hoshizaki|Moran|2001|p=238}} refer to the epithets bestowed by Poiret and Willdenow, respectively. Lellinger, who referred to the species as ''N.&nbsp;aurea'', called it '''golden cloak fern'''.{{sfn|Lellinger|1985|p=154}} Members of the genus ''Notholaena'' as historically defined are commonly known as "cloak ferns". The name of the genus is derived from Greek, meaning "false cloak", referring to the fact that the sori are not covered by well-differentiated tissue of the leaf margin.{{sfn|Clute|1901|pp=287}} It has also been called '''slender cloak fern'''.{{sfn|Hoshizaki|Moran|2001|p=238}}

Further molecular studies in ''Myriopteris'' demonstrated the existence of three well-supported [[clade]]s within the genus. ''M.&nbsp;aurea'' belongs to what Grusz ''et al.'' informally named the ''covillei'' clade. ''M.&nbsp;aurea'' and the similar ''M.&nbsp;yatskievychiana'' are [[sister taxon|sister]] to the rest of the clade; all other members of the clade, except [[Myriopteris newberryi|''M.&nbsp;newberryi'']], belong to the "core ''covillei''" clade, with leaves finely divided into bead-like segments, quite dissimilar to ''M.&nbsp;aurea''.{{sfn|Grusz|Windham|Yatskievych|Huiet|2014|pp=704–705}}

==Distribution and habitat==
[[File:Helecho Dorado en CDMX.jpg|alt=Rosette of fern fronds sprouting from a rough-barked tree|thumb|475x475px|Golden-lipped fern adjacent to and sprouting on the trunk of ''[[Fraxinus uhdei]]'' in an urban park in Mexico.]]
''Myriopteris aurea'' grows throughout Mexico except for [[Tabasco]] and the [[Yucatan Peninsula]], where it is the most widespread and abundant fern in the country.{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}}{{sfn|Rebman|Gibson|Rich|2016|p=19}} The range extends slightly north into the United States, into [[Arizona]], [[New Mexico]], and [[Trans-Pecos]] [[Texas]].{{sfn|Kartesz|2014}}

In the east, it is found among the [[Greater Antilles]] and in [[Venezuela]]. To the south, it extends through Central America into South America and along the [[Andes Mountains]],{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}} where it has been described as "one of the characteristic ferns of the [[Altiplano]]",{{sfn|Tryon|Stolze|1989|p=28}} as far south as [[Chile]] and [[Argentina]], and east into [[Brazil]], [[Paraguay]] and [[Uruguay]].{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}}

The species grows on dry, rocky slopes,{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}} and cliffs,{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}}{{sfn|Tryon|Stolze|1989|p=186}} soil banks, and shrubby hillsides.{{sfn|Tryon|Stolze|1989|p=28}}{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}} It tolerates a variety of different rocks, although it is comparatively uncommon on [[limestone]].{{sfn|Windham|Rabe|1993}} It is found at altitudes from {{convert|600|to|3800|m|ft|sigfig=2|sp=us}}.{{sfn|Mickel|Smith|2004|p=186}}{{sfn|Tryon|Stolze|1989|p=28}}{{sfn|Stolze|1981|p=319}}

==Ecology and conservation==
The species is globally secure (G5). [[NatureServe]] does not assign conservation rankings for the three states of the United States at the northern edge of its range.{{sfn|NatureServe|2026}}

==Uses and cultivation==
''Myriopteris aurea'' can be grown on moist-dry to dry garden soil, supplemented with sand. The soil must be well-drained and it requires high levels of light.{{sfn|Hoshizaki|Moran|2001|p=238}} The horticulturist George Schneider, writing in 1892, spoke of it as "an old inhabitant of our gardens".{{sfn|Schneider|1892|p=608}}

[[Edward Palmer (botanist)|Edward Palmer]] collected a specimen at the market in [[Saltillo]] in 1898, where he reported that a [[decoction]] was prepared from it and drunk to treat "pain in the stomach" and "coughs".{{sfn|Johnston|1943|pp=312–313}}

